# {{PROJECT_NAME}} — the project context repository

*For everyone on the project. Part 1 needs no technical background; Part 2 is for
people who work in the code repositories.* Repository owner: {{OWNER}}. {{DATE}}.

## Part 1 — For everyone

**Why it exists.** Project knowledge — scope, decisions and their reasons, what a word
means to the client, who answers for what — usually lives in chats, slides and heads.
Every newcomer, every hand-over and every AI assistant rediscovers it.
`{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-context` is the one place where **{{PROJECT_NAME}}** keeps *what is true
today*, kept current by named people. Nothing about scope, decisions or vocabulary is
authoritative anywhere else.

**What is in it.**

| Area | Answers |
| :-- | :-- |
| Baseline | What is the project held to? Scope, assumptions, risks, non-functional requirements. |
| Architecture | How is the system shaped, now and in the target state? |
| Product | What are we building and why? |
| Delivery | How is the project run? |
| Quality | How do we know it works? |
| Backend, Frontend, Design | How each delivery layer works, and what it has settled. |
| Decisions | Every decision, one folder, with a scope column. Only accepted ones bind. |
| Glossary | What does each term mean here? |

**Three principles.**

1. *State of today.* A document describes the current agreed state; history is kept by
   version control and decision records, not old copies. `DEPRECATED` at the top means
   history.
2. *Decisions are recorded once, with reasons.* What was decided, the alternatives, why
   they lost — all in one folder. Only `Accepted` records bind. This stops the same
   debate every quarter.
3. *One area, one owner.* The owner keeps the area true and approves every change to
   it. One person may own several areas; no area has two owners or none.

**How the AI assistant uses it.** When a task needs a project fact — a decision, a
term, an owner — the assistant reads this repository and names its source: the file,
when it last changed, and who owns it. It does not read it before every task, so ask
it directly when it matters ("what did we decide about payments?"). If it could not get
the latest version, it says so first.

**Your copy.** The assistant keeps a copy of this repository on your computer. The
first time, it asks where — a suggested folder, a copy you already have, or a folder you
pick. After that it refreshes the copy on its own, as long as you have access to the
repository on the git host.

**What happens on its own, and what never does.**

| On its own | Never without your "yes" |
| :-- | :-- |
| Refreshing your copy when it is on the main version and you have not changed anything in it | Sending any change to the git host |
| Telling you when it read a draft, an old copy or unsaved changes | Changing the main version — that only happens when the area owner approves a pull request |
| Installing the team's shared instructions for the assistant, after you trust the project folder once | Removing or editing the project pointer in a repository's `CLAUDE.md` |

**Changing something — no git needed.** Open the file in the git host's web page,
click *Edit*, describe the change. The host turns it into a *pull request*: a proposed
change the area's owner reviews and approves. Or ask the assistant ("write down that
we moved the release to Friday"): it asks before it starts, prepares the change in a
separate folder, shows it to you, and asks again before saving it and before sending
it. Either way, the change reaches everyone once the owner approves it.

**Messages you may see.**

| The assistant says | It means | Do |
| :-- | :-- | :-- |
| "Read from your branch / your unsaved changes" | Your copy holds a draft, not the approved version | Fine for your own drafts; for facts, ask the owner or finish the change |
| "Could not reach the server" | No network, VPN or access | Check access; the answer may be out of date |
| "History on the server differs" | Someone rewrote the main version | Say yes when the assistant offers a fresh copy; your old copy stays as it is |
| "A file blocks the update" | A file in your copy is in the way of the new version | Move that file away; the next question updates your copy |
| "Exists only on this machine" | The project has no address on the git host yet | Ask the repository owner to publish it |
| "The project knowledge base skill is unavailable" | The assistant's plugin is not installed | Ask your lead, or run the init skill's *Attach repository* |

**What it is not.** Not the backlog tool, not the chat, not the design tool, not the
code. It ties those together and points at them.

## Part 2 — For engineers

**Where things are.** This repository lives on the git host like any code repository.
Your clone of it can sit anywhere — beside the code repositories, in a project folder,
or in `~/.tsh/context/{{PROJECT_SLUG}}/`. The skills find it on their own: each code
repository carries the project's git URL in its settings, and the first skill run asks
once where your clone is or should be, then remembers it for every repository of the
project.

**Connecting a code repository.** Once per repository:
`/tsh-product-management:initializing-project-context` → *Attach repository*. It asks
whether the team may commit config to that repository:

| | A repository the team may commit to | A client's repository |
| :-- | :-- | :-- |
| Settings | `.claude/settings.json`, committed in a pull request | `.claude/settings.local.json`, never committed |
| Project pointer | A `tsh:project-context` block in `CLAUDE.md` | The same block in `CLAUDE.local.md` |
| Teammates | Get it with the next pull; accept the trust dialog once | Each runs *Attach repository* once |

Both files are kept out of git in the local case, so nothing reaches the client.
The pointer block must stay as written: no agent removes or edits it, and a hook in
`tsh-product-management` blocks attempts to.

**Reading.** `/tsh-product-management:navigating-project-context` — from a connected
repository, or anywhere with the URL as argument. Before reading it fetches at most
every ten minutes, never prompts for a password, and fast-forwards your clone only when
it is on the default branch with no uncommitted changes. On any other branch, or with
changes in progress, it reads what is there and says so. It never merges, rebases,
stashes, resets or switches branches.

**Writing.** `writing-project-knowledge`, `adding-project-context-workspace` and
`repairing-project-context-links` write in a separate worktree, on a new branch from the
approved version, beside your clone — never in your clone. Creating the branch,
committing and pushing it each wait for their own yes, and the pull request is left to
you. Nothing is ever pushed to the default branch. Decision records go to `docs/decisions/` here through
`/tsh-core:managing-decision-records`, never into a code repository.

**The shared plugin.** `.claude-plugin/marketplace.json` publishes
`plugins/{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-shared/`: Markdown skills and agents specific to this project
and needed by more than one role or repository — a release checklist, a domain review
guide. No hooks, servers or scripts; every change bumps `version`. Rule and procedure:
[plugins/{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-shared/README.md](plugins/{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-shared/README.md).

**The quality gate.** Four scripts in `scripts/`, run by `bitbucket-pipelines.yml` on
every pull request: links and kebab-case names, tables that render, unique decision
numbers, a Markdown-only and versioned plugin. On another host, run them in its
pipeline. Protect the default branch on the host — no direct pushes, one approval.

**Where a new skill goes.** Needed by every project the same way → the company
marketplace, not here. Needed by more than one role or repository of this project → this
plugin, named `{{PROJECT_SLUG}}-<what-it-does>`. One repository only → that repository's
`.claude/`. Ties go to the repository.
